@using Microsoft.Extensions.Localization
@using TrainingHub.Translations
@inject IStringLocalizer<CommonResources> L
<!DOCTYPE html>
@* The language is the request's, resolved by the localization middleware before any component
   renders — the cookie choice first, the browser's Accept-Language otherwise, English when
   neither says anything. Stamped here because this component renders on every route, so the
   attribute is both the document's honest label and the one place the WebAssembly boot reads
   the server's resolution back from (ADR 0088). *@
<html lang="@Language">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8"/>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"/>
    <base href="/"/>
    @* The theme, resolved before anything is painted, and the only place that resolves it.

       It has to run here because the catalog's routes prerender (ADR 0072, ADR 0074): the server
       sends a complete page painted with the light palette, the browser shows it at once, and C#
       cannot correct it until WebAssembly has booted. That is not a frame of flash — it is the
       whole time-to-interactive, on the three most public pages of the product.

       A stored choice wins; without one the device answers, so a visitor who never touched the
       toggle follows their system. The answer is stamped on the document element, and app.css
       paints from it (ADR 0077). Wrapped in a try because a browser that refuses storage must
       still get a page. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var stored = localStorage.getItem("theme");
                var dark = stored === "dark"
                    || (stored !== "light" && window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches);

                document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", dark ? "dark" : "light");
            } catch (error) {
                document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light");
            }
        })();
    </script>
    @if (!IsCatalogRoute)
    {
        @* The routes that do not prerender ship an empty head until WebAssembly boots; these two
           lines are what a browser tab and a link unfurler see in the meantime. The catalog's
           routes write their own head during the prerendered pass (ADR 0073), which is why they
           are excluded — a second title here would double theirs.

           The title is the product's name and stays as it is in every language; the description
           is a sentence and is read from CommonResources, in the culture the middleware just
           resolved (ADR 0091). *@
        <title>TrainingHub</title>
        <meta name="description" content="@L["SiteDescription"]"/>
    }
    @* Three faces, three jobs (the design's own vocabulary): Bricolage Grotesque carries the
       display voice, Source Sans 3 carries the body, and IBM Plex Mono carries the counts and
       labels — the machine's half of a catalog served by an engineering showcase. *@
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Bricolage+Grotesque:opsz,wght@12..96,600;12..96,700;12..96,800&family=Source+Sans+3:wght@400;600;700&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/>
    <link href=@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.css"] rel="stylesheet"/>
    <link href=@Assets["app.css"] rel="stylesheet"/>
    <ImportMap/>
@*
    Prerendering is decided here, per request, and the decision is ADR 0072's: the catalog's
    routes prerender, nothing else does. The root is one of them now, because the catalog is the
    front door — the same page answers both addresses (ADR 0074).

    The catalog's routes are the application's public face — the pages ADR 0062 opened to a
    visitor with no account, and the only ones a crawler is meant to read. They are pages of
    content: what they show is fetched in OnInitializedAsync and handed to the interactive pass
    through PersistentComponentState, so the first paint is the real page rather than a spinner,
    and the WebAssembly boot does not fetch it again.

    Everything else keeps prerender off, for the reason this comment has always carried: those
    pages are interactive MudBlazor controls behind a sign-in, and a prerendered pass renders them
    inert — a login form the user can see and type into but that silently drops the click until
    WebAssembly boots. There is also nobody to render them for: a crawler has no cookie.
*@
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="PageRenderMode"/>
</head>

<body>
<Routes @rendermode="PageRenderMode"/>
<script src="_framework/blazor.web.js"></script>
<script src=@Assets["_content/MudBlazor/MudBlazor.min.js"]></script>
@* Turnstile's loader, deferred: the widget itself is rendered explicitly, by the contact dialog,
   because the dialog appears long after this script has scanned the page (ADR 0083). *@
<script src="https://challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/api.js" defer></script>
</body>

</html>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The request being answered. Present here because this component renders statically, once
    /// per request — which is exactly why it is the one place that can decide per address.
    /// </summary>
    [CascadingParameter]
    public HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The resolved language, as the localization middleware left it on this request's thread.
    /// </summary>
    private static string Language => System.Globalization.CultureInfo.CurrentUICulture.Name;

    /// <summary>
    /// One mode for both outlets: <c>HeadOutlet</c> and <c>Routes</c> must agree, or the head's
    /// content is written twice.
    /// </summary>
    private InteractiveWebAssemblyRenderMode PageRenderMode =>
        new(prerender: IsCatalogRoute);

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the address is one of the catalog's routes: the root — the catalog is the front
    /// door (ADR 0074) — <c>/catalog</c> itself, or anything beneath it: the training's page and
    /// the trainer's profile (ADR 0062, ADR 0070).
    /// </summary>
    private bool IsCatalogRoute
    {
        get
        {
            var path = HttpContext?.Request.Path.Value;

            return path is not null
                   && (string.Equals(path, "/", StringComparison.Ordinal)
                       || string.Equals(path, "/catalog", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                       || path.StartsWith("/catalog/", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase));
        }
    }
}
